// =====================================================================
//  Grafana Alloy - TP Monitoring (log pipeline)
//
//  GOAL (Step 5): make Alloy TAIL the app's log file and SHIP the lines
//  to Loki, labelled so you can query them by `service` and `level`.
//
//  The app (TP1) writes its log to the shared volume at
//  /listings-logs/listings.log (LOGGING_FILE_NAME in
//  ../springboot/docker-compose.yml); Alloy mounts that volume read-only.
//  Loki push API: http://loki:3100/loki/api/v1/push
//
//  An Alloy config is a set of COMPONENTS wired together:
//      <component.type> "<label>" { arguments ... }
//  and a component reads another one's output by reference:
//      forward_to = [loki.write.loki.receiver]
//
//  The config below BOOTS cleanly (only the Loki writer, nothing feeds it).
//  In STEP 5 you add three components in front of it, in this order:
//      local.file_match  -> which file(s), with the labels `service` (+ job)
//      loki.source.file  -> tail those files
//      loki.process      -> a regex stage that extracts `level`,
//                           a labels stage that turns it into a label
//  ... and the last one forwards to loki.write.loki.receiver.
//
//  Reload after an edit:  curl -X POST http://localhost:12345/-/reload
//  Component graph (UI):  http://localhost:12345
// =====================================================================

// Ship to Loki (present so the service boots; your pipeline forwards here).
loki.write "loki" {
  endpoint {
    url = "http://loki:3100/loki/api/v1/push"
  }
}

// STEP 5 (student): add your components below. Example SHAPE:
//
// local.file_match "listings" {
